<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Former Liang</h1><p class="st-meta">320 CE – 376 CE · capital Guzang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Hexi Corridor state of the Zhang family, Chinese governors loyal in name to the Jin, which kept learning and trade alive in the far north-west for decades.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-gui">Zhang Gui</a>, a Chinese official, was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> province in 301 and governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> (modern Wuwei, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>). After the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a> his family ruled independently; the state is conventionally dated from 320, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-mao">Zhang Mao</a> took over after his brother's assassination.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Zhang rulers usually acknowledged the Jin emperors in the south and avoided imperial titles, governing as Chinese administrators. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu-corridor">Hexi Corridor</a> became a refuge for scholars and refugees from the chaos to the east, and the state profited from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> trade. In 327 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-jun-former-liang">Zhang Jun</a> set up <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gaochang-kingdom">Gaochang</a> commandery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a> basin.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 376 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fu-jian-337-385">Fu Jian</a> sent an army and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-tianxi">Zhang Tianxi</a> surrendered, taking a post at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> court. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river">Fei River</a> battle he fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin</a>. Hexi later passed to a series of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/liang-liang-shidu">Liang</a> states, including <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang-386-403">Later Liang</a> (386-403) and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-liang">Northern Liang</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/former-liang.jpg" alt="Former Liang" width="320" height="228"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_338_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>320 CE – 376 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Guzang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhang Gui, Zhang Jun, Zhang Tianxi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese ancestral religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liang (Zhang clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=348&amp;focus=former-liang" aria-label="Former Liang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7395 1003 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Former Liang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M7769 1191h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7715 1259h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7715 1259h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=348&amp;focus=former-liang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

